1. OBJECTS
An object is a part of a program It has data and it can do actions
Data in an object-properties 
Actions an object can do- methods
  : WHY
Programs use objects to do specific tasks
It breaks a big program into small parts
Most languages use object-oriented programming (OOP)
  : TYPES
Visible objects : controls  Label, Button, TextBox
Invisible objects : Timer, OpenFileDialog
Class = code that describes a type of object
  
    2. Forms and Controls
 What
Form = the window of your app. Form1 is created automatically and is empty
Properties = settings for how an object looks and behaves left = name, right = value
     = Why
Properties control the appearance. Example: Text changes the title bar 
     =Types 
Add a control: double-click it in the Toolbox, or drag it to the form
Change it: resize sizing handles edit properties
Delete it: select it, press the Delete key
Naming rules (identifiers): start with a letter or then letters, digits or  no spaces

  3. Introduction to C# Code
1. What
C# code is organized in three levels: namespace , class ,method
Event-driven = the program waits for a user action (event), then responds
2. Why
Organization keeps code clear. Event handlers make the app react to clicks, keys and mouse
3. Types
Namespace: container that holds classes
Class: container that holds methods
Method: group of statements that do an operation
